# livelist.keys -- exercises step 7 of build/plans/isocline.rst: the
# non-modal live completion list (ic_enable_completion_live_list()) and
# accept-on-space (ic_enable_completion_accept_on_space()).  Both are
# turned on only for this test, by jig.c's run_child_readline_loop()
# recognizing the "livelist" basename -- every other ".keys" test keeps
# the classic single-match hint behavior unchanged.
#
# Part 1 (list navigation, then ENTER with several candidates still
# listed): "show" matches all nine of the fake completer's candidates
# (every one starts with "show"), sorted alphabetically with "show
# arp" first and selected.  TAB/DOWN move forward, UP/SHIFT-TAB move
# back, all wrapping; one full lap (TAB, DOWN, UP, SHIFT-TAB) is a net
# no-op and lands back on "show arp".  ENTER is then pressed directly,
# with no RIGHT/END first: it must both materialize the selected
# candidate into the buffer and submit the line in the same keystroke.
i 2
+ show
k TAB
k DOWN
k UP
k SHIFT-TAB
w 20
k ENTER
w 20
#
# Part 2 (accept-on-space with a single remaining match): "show a"
# narrows the list to just "show arp".  With only one candidate its
# longest common prefix is the whole match, which is trivially a full
# token, so SPACE both completes it and adds the separator itself
# ("show arp "), exactly as the single-candidate case always did.  The
# list then has nothing to show for "show arp now" (no candidate
# starts with it), so the rest types in as ordinary self-insert and
# ENTER submits normally, with no hint pending.
+ show a
k SPACE
+ now
k ENTER
w 20
#
# Part 3 (ENTER completes and submits a single match): "show in"
# leaves only "show interfaces" matching, with its remaining text
# showing as a hint and the list collapsed to that one row.  ENTER
# again completes and submits in the same keystroke -- the same fix as
# part 1, now for a single candidate instead of nine.
+ show in
k ENTER
w 20
#
# Part 4 (accept-on-space with a genuinely ambiguous, non-full-token
# prefix): "show why mo" matches only "show why more or less" and
# "show why more works".  Their longest common prefix is "show why
# more " -- it already ends in the shared space from "more", but the
# very next character is still ambiguous ('o' vs 'w'), so it is not a
# full token.  SPACE extends the buffer to that common prefix and
# clears the list, but adds no space of its own, so the plain "x"
# typed right after lands after the single existing space, not two.
+ show why mo
k SPACE
+ x
k ENTER
w 20
#
# Confirm what actually got committed, most-recent first: index 0
# "show why more x" (part 4), index 1 "show interfaces" (part 3),
# index 2 "show arp now" (part 2), index 3 "show arp" (part 1).
k C-X
w 20
q
